// Comprehensive 7 Technical FAQs
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How does FastToolVerse convert images without uploading them to a remote server?",
    answer: "Unlike traditional cloud converters like Convertio or CloudConvert that transmit your private files to remote servers, FastToolVerse operates 100% inside your web browser. Utilizing HTML5 Canvas, OffscreenCanvas, TypedArray buffers, and client-side binary streams, your photos are decoded, resampled, and re-encoded locally in your computer's RAM. Zero bytes are ever transmitted over the network, allowing you to convert sensitive files even while completely disconnected from the internet."
  },
  {
    question: "Which format should I choose for modern website performance: WebP or AVIF?",
    answer: "Choose WebP if your priority is universal browser compatibility (over 98% worldwide coverage) combined with a reliable 25%–35% file size reduction compared to JPEG, full alpha transparency, and fast client-side encoding. Choose AVIF if you need absolute maximum compression (often 50% smaller than JPEG) with 10-bit and 12-bit wide-gamut HDR color support on modern Chromium, Safari 16+, and Firefox browsers. For broad web production, converting to WebP provides the safest combination of performance and backward compatibility."
  },
  {
    question: "Why do transparent areas turn black when converting PNG to JPG?",
    answer: "The JPEG specification does not include an alpha channel (transparency). When converting transparent 32-bit RGBA files (PNG, WebP, or SVG) to 24-bit RGB JPEG, each pixel's alpha value is discarded. In standard converters, transparent pixels with RGB values of (0,0,0) render as pitch black. FastToolVerse solves this by providing a Smart Transparency Fill control (defaulting to clean #FFFFFF white or custom HEX) that composites transparent pixels onto a solid background before encoding, eliminating dark artifacts."
  },
  {
    question: "Can I combine multiple converted images into a single multi-page PDF document?",
    answer: "Yes. When you choose PDF as your target format, FastToolVerse allows you to export individual photos as standalone PDF files or click 'Combine All into 1 PDF' to merge up to 15 queued images into a single multi-page PDF document. Our client-side PDF 1.4 generator embeds your resampled image frames into standard DCT streams without server latency, watermarks, or page restrictions."
  },
  {
    question: "Does converting JPG to PNG increase the image quality?",
    answer: "No. Converting a lossy JPEG to lossless PNG cannot restore visual details or high frequencies permanently discarded during the original JPEG compression. However, converting to PNG is useful when you plan to edit, annotate, or apply layered graphics to an image, as PNG prevents further generation loss that occurs every time a JPEG is re-saved."
  },
  {
    question: "Are EXIF camera metadata and GPS coordinates removed during conversion?",
    answer: "Yes. By default, FastToolVerse strips EXIF metadata (including GPS coordinates, camera model, exposure settings, and serial numbers) during canvas re-encoding. This enhances privacy and shaves unnecessary metadata bytes from your output files. You can toggle metadata preservation in the Advanced Options panel if you require camera tags for photography archiving."
  },
  {
    question: "Is there any file size limitation or daily throttle on batch image conversions?",
    answer: "No. Because all conversion computation runs locally using your device's CPU and GPU rather than costly remote server infrastructure, FastToolVerse imposes zero file size limits, zero hourly conversion quotas, and zero paywalls. You can convert high-resolution camera photos, heavy RAW/TIFF files, and batches of up to 15 images simultaneously with 100% privacy and zero server lag."
  }
];

