        <!-- Deep Dive 1: Chroma Subsampling -->
        <article class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-sm flex flex-col justify-between">
          <div>
            <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-blue-50 dark:bg-blue-950/50 text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center justify-center mb-5 border border-blue-100 dark:border-blue-900/50">
              <ToolIcon name="eye" class="w-5 h-5" />
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-3">
              Understanding Chroma Subsampling and Raster Compression
            </h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
              Human visual perception is substantially more sensitive to variations in brightness (luminance, <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">Y</code>) than to fine shifts in color hue and saturation (chrominance, <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">Cb</code> and <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">Cr</code>). Modern lossy encoders exploit this biological trait through <strong>4:2:0 chroma subsampling</strong>.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
              By sampling chrominance resolution at half horizontal and half vertical rates relative to luminance, 50% of raw color data is discarded before Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) quantization begins. This yields immediate 50% byte savings with virtually zero perceptible loss on photorealistic scenes.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-6 pt-4 border-t border-slate-100 dark:border-slate-800/80">
